  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\n1023 mb high pressure over the eastern Great Lakes and ridging\nsw into IL and providing fair skies with some thin cirrus clouds\nespecially western and northern IL. Patchy shallow ground fog\nnoted near the Wabash river valley, with Robinson airport down\nto 1.5 miles (varying from 1/4 to 5 miles) and Paris having a\nlight fog/haze at 5 miles. The latest CAMs are more aggressive\nwith fog formation in eastern IL where pockets of dense fog in\neast central IL from I-57 east through 12Z-13Z. Though fog\ncurrently not as widespread as HRRR and RAP13 are forecasting so\nmentioned patchy fog in eastern IL until 8 am with areas of fog\nnear the Wabash river/Indiana border. \n\nAn MCS over eastern SD, nw Iowa and central/sw MN will affect\nIowa and WI with showers/thunderstorms today with a weakening\noutflow boundary moving se into the IL river valley during this\nafternoon. HRRR was more aggressive with scattered showers\nduring mid/late afternoon nw of I-55 though drier lower levels\nwill likely dissipate these with sprinkles possible northern/nw\nCWA this afternoon. We will see increasing mid/high clouds from\nthe west/nw during today with few-scattered cumulus clouds also\nforming by midday/afternoon. South to SSE winds 5-15 mph today\nwith few gusts 15-20 mph at midday and afternoon. Highs 83-88F\ntoday, coolest ne of I-74 where more clouds expected today. \n\nWarm advection/isentropic lift increases tonight into Sunday\nmorning, as a warm front lifts ne across CWA overnight into\nSunday morning. Warm front to be near the WI/IL border by sunset\nSunday, with our CWA likely staying in warm sector much of the\nupcoming week. We have slight chance of convection over Knox \nand Stark counties overnight into early Sunday morning. Lows \novernight 65-70F and mildest over the IL river valley. \n\nMain weather story will be the extended heat wave to impact\ncentral and southeast IL much of the upcoming week. 500 mb\nheights of 588-590 decameters this morning to increase to\n594-596 decameters Monday through Wed High confidence remains \nin a late season thermal ridge amplifying across the Mid- \nMississippi Valley for much of next week. Deterministic and \nensemble guidance align on 500 mb heights swelling to 593-596 \ndam, with 850 mb temperatures ranging between +22C and +24C. \nExtreme Forecast Index (EFI) values from the ECMWF range between\n0.80 and 0.85 for max temperatures Monday through Wednesday and\nonly slightly lower late this week. A stronger mid/upper level\nridge near central IL would keep us hotter along with the \"ring\nof fire\" with convection from MCS's to generally stay north of\ncentral IL. Highs Sunday in the upper 80s/lower 90s and low to\nmid 90s Monday with heat indices peaking around 100F Mon\nafternoon, warmer over western CWA. Tue and Wed appear to be the\nhottest days with highs in the mid to upper 90s with areas from\nSpringfield to Lawrenceville getting close to 100F. Afternoon\nheat indices likely reach 100-105 and may be close to heat\nadvisory criteria then. The last time Springfield reached 100 \nor higher was July 31, 2012 when it was 101 and we were also in \na severe drought much of that summer. The Globe EF1 for ILX CWA \nshows 0.90 Monday, 0.97 Tue, 0.99 Wed and 0.94 Thu.\n\nAs upper level ridge starts to flatten and retrograde west late\nthis week, we could see low convection chances starting Thu and\ngoing into Labor Day Weekend. Still hot on Thu with highs in the\nlower 90s from I-74 ne (pm heat indices around 100) and upper \n90s over SW CWA and southeast IL (pm heat indices 100-105). \nHighs by next Saturday and Sunday (Sep 5-6th) in upper 80s and \nlower 90s which is still about 10 degrees warmer than normal for\nearly September. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Sep\n5-11 has around 60% chance of above normal temperatures over\ncentral/se IL with precipitation trending near normal. CPC Week\n3-4 Outlook for Sep 12-25 (release Aug 28) has equal chances of\nabove and below normal temperatures and precipitation.",
